# Targets chezmoi should DELETE from the destination on apply.
#
# Deleting a file from the source tree only stops chezmoi managing it — the
# rendered copy stays on every machine that already applied it. This file is how
# a retired thing actually leaves those machines. Entries are idempotent: a path
# that isn't there is a no-op, so they're safe to leave in place indefinitely.
#
# BE CAREFUL. Anything listed here is removed from $HOME on the next apply, on
# every machine. Only list files this repo used to own.

# crush-signal-channel — retired 2026-07-24, superseded by the harness in
# ~/.config/harness/harness.toml (seeded as `crush-glm`, renamed `crush-signal`
# on 2026-07-25 — see below; the near-identical names are a coincidence of both
# describing the same job). The tmux + launchd/systemd wrapper
# around a headless Crush arbitrating the Signal channel is now harness's job
# (supervision, restart, attach), so the bespoke supervisor is dead weight.
# NOTE: the data dir ~/.local/share/crush-signal-channel is deliberately NOT
# listed — it holds that agent's session history and logs. Delete it by hand if
# and when you want it gone.
.local/bin/crush-signal-channel.sh
.oh-my-zsh/custom/crush-signal-channel.zsh
Library/LaunchAgents/rocks.stump.crush-signal-channel.plist
.config/systemd/user/crush-signal-channel.service

# zsh-harnessd — retired 2026-07-25, superseded by the Go `harness` binary
# (gitea.stump.rocks/stump.wtf/harness, installed via go-tools.txt). The plugin
# was still being cloned as an external on every machine but was never listed in
# `plugins=()` in dot_zshrc, so nothing sourced it — a ~200KB clone and a pull on
# every apply for a plugin no shell ever loaded. Supervision, attach and profiles
# all live in `harness` now; config is ~/.config/harness/harness.toml.
# The legacy zsh-harnessd.service on Linux nodes is stopped/disabled separately
# by .chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_after_51-harness-daemon-service.sh.tmpl.
.oh-my-zsh/custom/plugins/zsh-harnessd

# crush-glm — renamed 2026-07-25 to `crush-signal`, which says what the harness
# IS (the Signal-driven crush) rather than which model it happens to pin; the
# claude-code harness landed alongside it and the pair needed parallel names.
# Only the env_file is listed: harness.toml itself is `create_` (the TUI owns it,
# so chezmoi must not touch it — rename the [harness.*] table by hand or in the
# TUI on machines that already have it), and ~/.local/share/crush-glm is a data
# dir holding that agent's session history — `mv` it to crush-signal to keep the
# history, or leave it and let a fresh pin be seeded.
.config/harness/crush-glm.env

# zsh-harnessd helper scripts — the harness-run symlink and the standalone
# claude-headless wrapper from the tmux era. Their units/LaunchAgents are torn
# down by run_onchange_after_51-harness-daemon-service, which also rm -f's
# these; listing them here covers machines where that script has already fired.
.local/bin/harness-run
.local/bin/claude-headless.sh

# stumpcloud-sweep standalone schedulers — retired when the sweep moved into a
# scheduled [harness.stumpcloud-sweep] entry in ~/.config/harness/harness.toml
# (harness grew native `schedule` support). The units are stopped/disabled by
# .chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_after_52-harness-reload.sh.tmpl; these entries
# remove the files from machines that already applied them.
.config/systemd/user/stumpcloud-sweep.service
.config/systemd/user/stumpcloud-sweep.timer
Library/LaunchAgents/rocks.stump.stumpcloud-sweep.plist
